<BaseLayout title="BL40 (Weizhong) — Bend Middle Point" description="Learn how to locate and stimulate BL40 (Weizhong) — the Bend Middle point. Benefits include back pain relief, sciatica support, and knee pain relief." breadcrumbs={[{ label: "Home", href: "/" }, { label: "Points", href: "/points/" }, { label: "BL40 (Weizhong)", href: "/points/bl40-weizhong-acupressure-point/" }]} datePublished="2025-03-01" dateModified="2026-08-19" articleSchema={true}>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 lg:px-6">
    <h1 class="mb-4">BL40 (Weizhong) — Bend Middle</h1>
    <p class="text-lg mb-8 text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">BL40, known as Weizhong or Bend Middle, is located at the back of the knee and is a major point for acute lower back pain, sciatica, and knee discomfort.</p>

    <section class="mb-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">At a Glance</h2>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 overflow-hidden">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-stone-100 dark:divide-stone-800">
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Meridian</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Bladder (BL)</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Body region</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Center of the crease behind the knee (popliteal fossa)</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Best for</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Acute lower back pain, sciatica, knee pain and weak legs</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Difficulty</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Easy to reach with both thumbs; a massage stick or tennis ball helps if you are stiff</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Hold time</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">30–60 seconds per leg</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Frequency</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">1–3 times daily</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Pairs well with</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300"><a href="/points/gb34-yanglingquan-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">GB34 (Yanglingquan)</a>, <a href="/points/bl60-kunlun-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">BL60 (Kunlun)</a>, <a href="/points/st36-zusanli-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">ST36 (Zusanli)</a></td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Skip if</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Varicose veins or a history of blood clots behind the knee, broken skin, or recent knee surgery (see Precautions)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Why BL40 Matters</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">BL40 sits in the crease behind the knee, far from the area it treats, yet it is widely regarded as the most important acupressure point for lower back pain. The Bladder meridian runs the length of the back and down the leg, connecting the popliteal crease directly to the lower back.</p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">Its name, Weizhong, meaning Bend Middle, refers to its position in the center of the bend behind the knee. In clinical tradition it is one of the most frequently used points for acute lower back strain, and it appears routinely in back-pain protocols — which is why a point so far from the lower back is worth learning even if you only press it occasionally.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Location</h2>
    <img src="/images/bl40-location.webp" alt="BL40 acupressure point location behind the knee" class="w-full max-w-sm mx-auto rounded-xl shadow-sm" width="1242" height="828" loading="lazy">
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">BL40 is at the back of the knee, in the exact center of the popliteal crease — the horizontal skin crease behind the knee joint. To find it, sit with the knee slightly bent so the crease is visible, then place a thumb in the center of that crease, midway between the two hamstring tendons.</p>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3"><strong>Three quick confirmations you have the right spot:</strong> (1) it is the midpoint of the crease, not the inner or outer edge of the knee; (2) pressing it with the knee slightly bent produces a deep, dull ache rather than a sharp or superficial sensation; (3) it usually sits in a slight hollow between the hamstring tendons — if you feel only bone or tendon, you are slightly off-center.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">When to Use BL40 (and When Not)</h2>
    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-3">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-forest-200 bg-forest-50 dark:border-forest-900/40 dark:bg-forest-900/20 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-2 text-sm">Use it when</h3>
        <ul class="space-y-1.5 text-sm text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
          <li>• You have sudden or nagging lower back pain, especially from a strain</li>
          <li>• Sciatica — pain radiating from the lower back down the back of the leg</li>
          <li>• Your knees feel stiff or your legs feel weak or heavy</li>
          <li>• Your hamstrings or calves are tight or cramping</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-stone-50 dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-800/50 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-2 text-sm">Hold off if</h3>
        <ul class="space-y-1.5 text-sm text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
          <li>• You can see varicose veins behind the knee, or you have a history of blood clots (DVT)</li>
          <li>• There is broken skin, a rash, or swelling in the popliteal area</li>
          <li>• You recently had knee surgery, a knee injury, or a Baker's cyst behind the knee</li>
          <li>• Back pain comes with numbness, weakness, or loss of bowel or bladder control — get urgent care</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">Use the <em>Hold off</em> column as a safety filter: BL40 is a comfort practice for back and leg tension — never press hard over veins, and acute red-flag back pain needs urgent medical care.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Benefits &amp; Uses</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-3 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li><strong>Lower back pain:</strong> BL40 is considered one of the most effective points for acute lower back strain and discomfort.</li>
      <li><strong>Sciatica:</strong> It helps relieve radiating leg pain that follows the sciatic pathway from the lower back.</li>
      <li><strong>Knee pain and weak legs:</strong> It eases stiffness and discomfort in the knee and can help legs that feel heavy or unsteady.</li>
      <li><strong>Hamstring and calf tension:</strong> It relaxes tight hamstrings and calves and helps with leg cramps.</li>
      <li><strong>Post-lifting recovery:</strong> Pressed after a day of standing, lifting, or heavy activity, it helps release the pull that runs from the lower back down the back of the leg.</li>
    </ul>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">How to Massage BL40</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">Press with the knee slightly bent so the crease is soft and relaxed — pressing with the knee locked straight makes the point hard to reach and uncomfortable. Both thumbs on the center of the crease works best; if it is awkward, a massage stick, a tennis ball against a wall, or a partner can help.</p>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li>Sit with your knee slightly bent and leg relaxed.</li>
      <li>Place your thumbs on the BL40 point behind your knee.</li>
      <li>Apply firm, steady pressure with a small circular motion.</li>
      <li>Hold for 30–60 seconds, then gently straighten and bend your leg.</li>
      <li>Repeat on the other leg as needed, 1–3 times daily.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="mt-3 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed"><strong>Common mistake to avoid:</strong> pressing hard over visible veins, or pressing with the knee fully straight. The target sensation is a deep, dull ache in the middle of the crease — if you feel a sharp or electric twinge down the leg, or you are pressing over varicose veins, ease off.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Combine BL40 with These Points</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">A full back-and-leg routine is more effective than pressing one point. Pick the row closest to your situation and work the points in the order shown, 30–60 seconds each.</p>
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 overflow-hidden">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-stone-100 dark:divide-stone-800">
          <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-44">Goal / symptom</th><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">Routine (in order)</th></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Acute lower back strain</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">BL40 → <a href="/points/gb34-yanglingquan-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">GB34</a>, gentle, 2–3 times daily</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Sciatica / radiating leg pain</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">BL40 → GB34 → <a href="/points/bl60-kunlun-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">BL60</a>, working down the leg, 30–60s each</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Knee pain and stiff legs</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">BL40 → <a href="/points/st36-zusanli-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">ST36</a>, then gentle knee bends</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">After a day of standing or lifting</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">BL40 + ST36, 30–60s each, then a short walk</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">For a fuller program, see the <a href="/body/leg-acupressure-points/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">leg acupressure points</a> body guide, and pair with our <a href="/points/gb34-yanglingquan-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">GB34 (Yanglingquan)</a> and <a href="/points/bl60-kunlun-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">BL60 (Kunlun)</a> point guides for leg pain that runs down the outside or back of the leg.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">What to Expect</h2>
    <div class="space-y-3">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-1 text-sm">First session (today)</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">BL40 is often tender, and pressing it produces a deep, dull ache. Many people feel the lower back "let go" a little within a few minutes, though acute strain can return as the day goes on. Do not expect a cure after one press.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-1 text-sm">Days 3–7 (consistent daily practice)</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">With 1–2 sessions per day, tenderness at the point usually eases and acute lower back strain often settles. This matches the pattern in studies of acupressure for low back pain, where benefits appear after several days of regular use.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-1 text-sm">Weeks 2–4</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">For chronic or recurring back pain, pair BL40 with movement, stretching, and — ideally — a physiotherapy plan. Acupressure is a complement, not a replacement. No improvement after 2–3 weeks, or any red-flag symptoms, means seeing a clinician.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Science &amp; Research</h2>
    <p class="mb-3">Lower back pain is one of the most studied areas in acupressure research. Systematic reviews and randomized controlled trials have examined acupressure as an adjunct for back pain, with results that are generally promising but limited in quality. Meta-analyses of acupressure for pain tend to show a positive direction of effect, though study samples are often small and methods vary.</p>
    <p class="mb-3">Like other manual therapies, acupressure is hard to blind, so the specific contribution of a point such as BL40 is difficult to separate from the general effects of touch, pressure, and expectation. The evidence base is encouraging but not definitive — we lay out what is known, and what is still missing, in our <a href="/research/does-acupressure-work/">does acupressure work?</a> review.</p>

    <EvidenceBox refs={["chen-wang-2014"]} note="The review above evaluated acupressure for pain, including the low-back and leg complaints this point is most used for. It is not a study of BL40 in isolation." />

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Precautions</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-3 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li>Avoid deep pressure over varicose veins, and never press if you have a history of blood clots (DVT).</li>
      <li>Do not press if there is broken skin, a rash, or swelling behind the knee.</li>
      <li>Avoid the point after recent knee surgery, a Baker's cyst, or an acute knee injury.</li>
      <li>Use gentle pressure on the elderly or anyone with fragile skin.</li>
      <li>Stop if you feel sharp or shooting pain down the leg.</li>
    </ul>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Red Flags — See a Professional</h2>
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-red-200 bg-red-50 dark:border-red-900/40 dark:bg-red-900/20 p-4 text-sm">
      <p class="text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300 mb-2">Stop self-treatment and see a doctor immediately if you notice any of the following:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
        <li>Sudden severe back pain with numbness, weakness, or pins-and-needles in the legs</li>
        <li>Loss of bowel or bladder control, or numbness in the groin or saddle area — get emergency care</li>
        <li>New swelling, warmth, or redness in the calf (possible blood clot — do not massage the area)</li>
        <li>Pain down the leg that is getting worse</li>
        <li>Back pain that has not improved after 2–3 weeks of regular acupressure</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Why does a point behind the knee help lower back pain?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">BL40 has a direct meridian connection to the lower back through the Bladder meridian, and it is considered one of the most effective points for acute lower back strain and one of the most frequently selected points in back-pain protocols. In practice, pressing it often produces a deep ache that seems to "release" tension running up into the back.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">How do I press BL40 if I cannot reach it?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Sit with the knee slightly bent and press both thumbs into the center of the crease for 30–60 seconds per leg. If it is awkward to reach alone, use a massage stick, a tennis ball pressed against a wall, or a partner — just keep pressure moderate and avoid pressing directly over visible veins.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">How long and how often should I press BL40?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Hold for 30–60 seconds per bout, then gently straighten and bend the leg, and repeat 1–3 times a day. Stop immediately if you feel sharp or shooting pain down the leg — that is a sign to lighten the pressure or skip the point.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Is BL40 safe if I have varicose veins?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Avoid deep pressure over varicose veins, and do not press at all if you have a history of blood clots (DVT) behind the knee. If you are unsure whether the veins behind your knee are safe to press, ask a clinician first — this is the main reason BL40 needs more caution than most points.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">When should I see a professional instead?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Get emergency care for sudden severe back pain with numbness or weakness in the legs, or loss of bowel or bladder control. See a doctor for new calf swelling, warmth, or redness (possible blood clot), for pain that keeps getting worse, or for back pain that has not improved after 2–3 weeks of regular acupressure.</div>
    </details>
    </section>

    <div class="mt-10 text-center">
      <a href="/points/" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-sm text-stone-500 hover:text-forest-600 dark:text-stone-400 dark:hover:text-forest-300">Back to All Points</a>
